#: source/store/resolution.md:1
msgid "Derivation Resolution"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:3
msgid ""
"_See [this chapter's appendix](../store/math-notation.md) on grammar and "
"metavariable conventions._"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:5
msgid ""
"To _resolve_ a derivation is to replace its [inputs](../store/derivation/"
"index.md#inputs) with the simplest inputs — plain store paths — that denote "
"the same store objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:7
msgid ""
"Derivations that only have store paths as inputs are likewise called "
"_resolved derivations_. (They are called that whether they are in fact the "
"output of derivation resolution, or just made that way without non-store-"
"path inputs to begin with.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:10
msgid "Input Content Equivalence of Derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:12
msgid ""
"[Deriving paths](../store/derivation/index.md#deriving-path) intentionally "
"make it possible to refer to the same [store object](../store/store-object."
"md) in multiple ways. This is a consequence of content-addressing, since "
"different derivations can produce the same outputs, and the same data can "
"also be manually added to the store. This is also a consequence even of "
"input-addressing, as an output can be referred to by derivation and output "
"name, or directly by its [computed](./derivation/outputs/input-address.md) "
"store path. Since dereferencing deriving paths is thus not injective, it "
"induces an equivalence relation on deriving paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:17
msgid ""
"Let's call this equivalence relation \\\\(\\\\sim\\\\), where \\\\(p_1 \\"
"\\sim p_2\\\\) means that deriving paths \\\\(p_1\\\\) and \\\\(p_2\\\\) "
"refer to the same store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:19
msgid ""
"**Content Equivalence**: Two deriving paths are equivalent if they refer to "
"the same store object:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:21
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{${}^\\*p_1 = {}^\\*p_2$} \\"
"\\UnaryInfC{$p_1 \\\\,\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{DP}\\\\, p_2$} \\\\end{prooftree} "
"\\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:28
msgid ""
"where \\\\({}^\\*p\\\\) denotes the store object that deriving path \\\\(p\\"
"\\) refers to."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:30
msgid "This also induces an equivalence relation on sets of deriving paths:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:32
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$\\\\{ {}^\\*p | p \\\\in P_1 \\\\} "
"= \\\\{ {}^\\*p | p \\\\in P_2 \\\\}$} \\\\UnaryInfC{$P_1 \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\"
"\\mathcal{P}(\\\\mathrm{DP})}\\\\, P_2$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:39
msgid ""
"**Input Content Equivalence**: This, in turn, induces an equivalence "
"relation on derivations: two derivations are equivalent if their inputs are "
"equivalent, and they are otherwise equal:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:41
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$\\\\mathrm{inputs}(d_1) \\\\,\\"
"\\sim\\_{\\\\mathcal{P}(\\\\mathrm{DP})}\\\\, \\\\mathrm{inputs}(d_2)$} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$ d\\_1\\left\\[\\\\mathrm{inputs} := \\\\{\\\\}\\right\\] = "
"d\\_2\\left\\[\\\\mathrm{inputs} := \\\\{\\\\}\\right\\] $} \\"
"\\BinaryInfC{$d_1 \\\\,\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\, d_2$} \\"
"\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:53
msgid ""
"Derivation resolution always maps derivations to input-content-equivalent "
"derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:55
msgid "Resolution relation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:57
msgid ""
"Dereferencing a derived path — \\\\({}^\\*p\\\\) above — was just introduced "
"as a black box. But actually it is a multi-step process of looking up build "
"results in the [build trace](../store/build-trace.md) that itself depends on "
"resolving the lookup keys. Resolution is thus a recursive multi-step process "
"that is worth diagramming formally."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:61
msgid ""
"We can do this with a small-step binary transition relation; let's call it \\"
"\\(\\rightsquigarrow\\\\). We can then conclude dereferenced equality like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:64
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$p\\_1 \\\\rightsquigarrow^\\* p$} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$p\\_2 \\\\rightsquigarrow^\\* p$} \\\\BinaryInfC{${}^\\*p\\_1 = {}"
"^\\*p\\_2$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:72
msgid ""
"I.e. by showing that both original items resolve (over 0 or more small "
"steps, hence the \\\\({}^\\*\\\\)) to the same exact item."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:74
msgid ""
"With this motivation, let's now formalize a [small-step](https://en."
"wikipedia.org/wiki/Operational_semantics#Small-step_semantics) system of "
"reduction rules for resolution."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:76
msgid "Formal rules"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:78
msgid "\\\\(\\text{resolved}\\\\) unary relation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:80
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$s \\in \\text{store-path}$} \\"
"\\UnaryInfC{$s$ resolved} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:87
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$\\forall i \\in \\mathrm{inputs}"
"(d). i \\text{ resolved}$} \\\\UnaryInfC{$d$ resolved} \\\\end{prooftree} \\"
"\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:94
msgid "\\\\(\\rightsquigarrow\\\\) binary relation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:96 source/store/resolution.md:148
msgid "**Remark**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:98
msgid ""
"Actually, to be completely formal we would need to keep track of the build "
"trace we are choosing to resolve against."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:100
msgid ""
"We could do that by making \\\\(\\rightsquigarrow\\\\) a ternary relation, "
"which would pass the build trace to itself until it finally uses it in that "
"one rule. This would add clutter more than insight, so we didn't bother to "
"write it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:103
msgid ""
"There are other options too, like saying the whole reduction rule system is "
"parameterized on the build trace, essentially [currying](https://en."
"wikipedia.org/wiki/Currying) the ternary \\\\(\\rightsquigarrow\\\\) into a "
"function from build traces to the binary relation written above."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:105
msgid "Core build trace lookup rule"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:107
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$s \\in \\text{store-path}$} \\"
"\\AxiomC{${}^\\*s \\in \\text{derivation}$} \\\\AxiomC{${}^\\*s$ resolved} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$\\mathrm{build\\text{-}trace}\\[s\\]\\[o\\] = t$} \\"
"\\QuaternaryInfC{$(s, o) \\rightsquigarrow t$} \\\\RightLabel{\\\\scriptsize "
"output path resolution} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:118
msgid "Inductive rules"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:120
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$i \\\\rightsquigarrow i'$} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$i \\\\in \\\\mathrm{inputs}(d)$} \\\\BinaryInfC{$d \\"
"\\rightsquigarrow d\\[i \\\\mapsto i'\\]$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:128
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$d \\\\rightsquigarrow d'$} \\"
"\\UnaryInfC{$(\\\\mathrm{path}(d), o) \\\\rightsquigarrow (\\\\mathrm{path}"
"(d'), o)$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:135
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$p \\\\rightsquigarrow p'$} \\"
"\\UnaryInfC{$(p, o) \\\\rightsquigarrow (p', o)$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:142
msgid "Properties"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:144
msgid ""
"Like all well-behaved evaluation relations, partial resolution is "
"[_confluent_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Confluence_(abstract_rewriting)). Also, if we take the symmetric closure of "
"\\\\(\\\\rightsquigarrow^\\*\\\\), we end up with the equivalence relations "
"of the previous section. Resolution respects content equivalence for "
"deriving paths, and input content equivalence for derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:150
msgid ""
"We chose to define from scratch an \"resolved\" unary relation explicitly "
"above. But it can also be defined as the normal forms of the \\\\(\\"
"\\rightsquigarrow^\\*\\\\) relation:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:153
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ a \\text{ resolved} \\Leftrightarrow \\forall b. b "
"\\rightsquigarrow^\\* a \\Rightarrow b = a\\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:155
msgid ""
"In prose, resolved terms are terms which \\\\(\\\\rightsquigarrow^\\*\\\\) "
"only relates on the left side to the same term on the right side; they are "
"the terms which can be resolved no further."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:157
msgid "Partial versus Complete Resolution"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:159
msgid ""
"Similar to evaluation, we can also speak of _partial_ versus _complete_ "
"derivation resolution. Partial derivation resolution is what we've actually "
"formalized above with \\\\(\\\\rightsquigarrow^\\*\\\\). Complete resolution "
"is resolution ending in a resolved term (deriving path or derivation). "
"(Which is a normal form of the relation, per the remark above.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:164
msgid ""
"With partial resolution, a derivation is related to equivalent derivations "
"with the same or simpler inputs, but not all those inputs will be plain "
"store paths. This is useful when the input refers to a floating content "
"addressed output we have not yet built — we don't know what (content-"
"address) store path will used for that derivation, so we are \"stuck\" "
"trying to resolve the deriving path in question. (In the above "
"formalization, this happens when the build trace is missing the keys we wish "
"to look up in it.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:168
msgid ""
"Complete resolution is a _functional_ relation, i.e. values on the left are "
"uniquely related with values on the right. It is not however, a _total_ "
"relation (in general, assuming arbitrary build traces). This is discussed in "
"the next section."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:172
msgid "Termination"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:174
msgid ""
"For static derivations graphs, complete resolution is indeed total, because "
"it always terminates for all inputs. (A relation that is both total and "
"functional is a function.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:177
msgid ""
"For [dynamic](../development/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-dynamic-"
"derivations) derivation graphs, however, this is not the case — resolution "
"is not guaranteed to terminate. The issue isn't rewriting deriving paths "
"themselves: a single rewrite to normalize an output deriving path to a "
"constant one always exists, and always proceeds in one step. The issue is "
"that dynamic derivations (i.e. those that are filled-in the graph by a "
"previous resolution) may have more transitive dependencies than the original "
"derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:182
msgid "**Example**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:184
msgid "Suppose we have this deriving path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:187 source/store/resolution.md:188
#: source/store/resolution.md:198
msgid "\"drvPath\""
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:188
msgid "\"...-foo.drv\""
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:189 source/store/resolution.md:191
#: source/store/resolution.md:199
msgid "\"output\""
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:189
msgid "\"bar.drv\""
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:191 source/store/resolution.md:199
msgid "\"baz\""
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:194
msgid ""
"and derivation `foo` is already resolved. When we resolve deriving path "
"we'll end up with something like."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:198
msgid "\"...-foo-bar.drv\""
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:202
msgid ""
"So far is just an atomic single rewrite, with no termination issues. But the "
"derivation `foo-bar` may have its _own_ dynamic derivation inputs. "
"Resolution must resolve that derivation first before the above deriving path "
"can finally be normalized to a plain `...-foo-bar-baz` store path."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:206
msgid ""
"The important thing to notice is that while \"build trace\" _keys_ must be "
"resolved. The _value_ those keys are mapped to have no such constraints. An "
"arbitrary store object has no notion of being resolved or not. But, an "
"arbitrary store object can be read back as a derivation (as will in fact be "
"done in case for dynamic derivations / nested output deriving paths). And "
"those derivations need _not_ be resolved."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:212
msgid ""
"It is those dynamic non-resolved derivations which are the source of non-"
"termination. By the same token, they are also the reason why dynamic "
"derivations offer greater expressive power."
msgstr ""
